===== Returning to OEM firmware =====
This device does not have TFTP in the bootloader by default, meaning there is no easy way to unbrick or install stock firmware without opening the device.

Despite this TP-Link have included a failsafe mode with a web interface for this device that can be triggered manually through serial or should the bootloader fail to boot the system.

==== Using serial port (requires opening the device) ====
  - Open the device up and wire up your serial adapter to the serial pads (see images)
  - Reboot the device
  - When the device is booting a bootloader prompt will appear with options
  - Press **x** to enter recovery
  - The recovery web interface will be available at http://192.168.0.1
  - Load a factory image into the web interface and install it

==== Causing the device to fail to boot on purpose (doesn't require opening the device) ====
:!: Warning, doing this wrong may brick the device and require much more complicated jtag flashing to recover. Be careful with the mtd command!

This requires the device already be running OpenWrt or other linux.

  - SSH into the device
  - Erase the **firmware** partition. ''mtd erase firmware''. This erases the partition in which OpenWrt resides causing the system to fail to boot and the bootloader to fallback into failsafe mode.
  - Reboot the device
  - The recovery web interface will be available at http://192.168.0.1
  - Load a factory image into the web interface and install it

In both cases it may be needed to set a static IP within the same network before one can reach the web interface, eg ''192.168.0.2/24''.

